# Line-ending policy — PHV5-002 (acceptance-matrix row A1)
#
# WHY: the reference development host runs core.autocrlf=true. Without these rules a fresh
# clone materializes executable scripts with CRLF, and a CRLF shebang line makes the interpreter
# unresolvable ("/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter", "env: 'node\r': No such file or directory").
# The hook guards and the test suite are executable scripts — `.sh` AND `.js` — so this is a
# silent, host-dependent breakage of the plugin's safety layer.
#
# That sentence named only shell scripts until CR-3 (2026-07-29). G0 selected lane N, which
# ships the guards as node scripts, and the policy silently stopped covering the files it exists
# to protect. `tests/layer1-config-wiring.sh` now asserts this rationale names both extensions,
# so it cannot go stale that way again.
#
# VERIFY (falsifying form — `git ls-files --eol` is NOT sufficient; it reports w/lf even with
# no .gitattributes present, so it passes before and after the fix):
#   git clone <repo> /tmp/eol-check && file /tmp/eol-check/scripts/*.sh
#   -> must NOT report "with CRLF line terminators"

# Shell scripts must be LF on every platform, always.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Node scripts, same requirement (CR-3). Lane N runs the hook guards as `node scripts/dg-*.js`,
# where the shebang is decorative and CRLF is harmless — but the guards are also executable
# directly, `tests/codex-challenge-test.js` runs Layer 5's 41 assertions, and Wave 6's
# determinism check (PHV5-062: clean clone -> regenerate -> byte-identical) must not be
# host-dependent. Covered on the same reasoning as *.sh rather than waiting for a failure.
*.js text eol=lf

# Test fixtures are DATA READ BY those shell scripts — tab-separated files compared with
# `cut -f2` / `grep -qxF`. A trailing \r makes every string comparison fail silently: no
# error, just wrong answers (a fresh clone reported 5 orphan files instead of 2). Same
# core.autocrlf landmine as above, quieter failure mode. See changes/CR-2.md.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Wave 0 snapshots are an immutable historical record whose SHA-256 hashes are recorded in
# snapshots/LEDGER-HEADER.md and are the sole input to the Wave 6 reconciliation. Any line-ending
# normalization would change their bytes and invalidate every recorded hash. Freeze them: no
# conversion in either direction.
docs/plans/2026-07-20-plugin-hardening-v5/snapshots/** -text
